QB.add(27,[{"id":27001,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Approximately what proportion of all medicines interact with G protein-coupled receptors?","o":["About 40%","About 4%","About 90%","About 10%","About 60%"],"e":"~<b>40%</b> of all medicines interact with GPCRs (“or maybe a little more”), making GPCRs a really important communication mechanism. Pathology often involves chemical signals gone wrong; medicines mimic or block signals; understanding signals explains drug effects and side effects.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"~40% of drugs act on GPCRs.","rn":[["","What does GPCR stand for?","What does GPCR stand for?","G protein-coupled receptor"],["","Why do drugs sometimes have unwanted effects?","Why do drugs sometimes have unwanted effects?","Different cells respond differently to the same signal"],["","How does acetylcholine act on a salivary gland cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a salivary gland cell?","Binds M1 receptor (GPCR, a G protein-coupled receptor); Causes secretion"]]},{"id":27002,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"“We do not create pathways for the drugs we take.” What does this mean?","o":["Drugs only use what the body already has","Drugs create new receptors that the body lacked","Drugs create new pathways that bypass receptors","Drugs replace hormones with new signals","Drugs only work in bacteria"],"e":"Paracetamol, ibuprofen and prescribed drugs use only what is <b>already in your body</b> – restoring a lost communication or bringing it to the right level. Drugs <b>augment</b> or <b>inhibit</b> existing signalling.","w":["They use existing receptors.","They use existing pathways.","They mimic or restore.","Not."],"k":"Drugs use existing pathways.","rn":[["","What do drugs do to signalling that is already happening?","What do drugs do to signalling that is already happening?","Augment it; Or inhibit it for a while"]]},{"id":27003,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"A patient cannot feel heat or pain when touching a hot plate. How is this best framed in signalling terms?","o":["A pathology where signalling to the brain is interrupted","A pathology where signalling to the brain is excessive","Normal physiology after repeated exposure","A drug side effect only","Positive feedback"],"e":"Removing your hand from a hot plate needs a signal to the brain; a pathology is when people cannot feel temperature/pain – the <b>signalling is interrupted</b>.","w":["Interrupted.","Not normal.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Interrupted signalling = pathology."},{"id":27004,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"Which of the following is <b>not</b> a reason cells communicate listed?","o":["To replicate their DNA identically","To respond to the immediate environment, including danger signals","To respond to central and local commands","To integrate signals from multiple sources","To induce or decrease growth if needed"],"e":"Reasons: respond to the immediate environment (danger signals), central commands, local commands; respond accordingly; coordinate responses; integrate signals; induce/decrease growth. When we don't respond accordingly → pathology.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Listed."],"k":"Reasons cells communicate."},{"id":27005,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"What does the bone marrow stem cell example illustrate?","o":["Signals must drive stem cell division and direct differentiation","Stem cells divide and differentiate at random, without signals","Stem cells never respond to signals once in the marrow","Bone marrow contains no stem cells","Stem cells only die when signalled"],"e":"Bone marrow stem cells divide through life; some differentiate (erythrocytes, macrophages, platelets). When needed they must <b>upregulate proliferation</b> and <b>differentiate correctly</b> – “Good communication is KEY!”","w":["Signals direct them.","They respond.","It does.","Not."],"k":"Stem cells depend on signals.","rn":[["","Bone marrow stem cells: what happens when new cells are needed?","Bone marrow stem cells: what happens when new cells are needed?","Upregulate proliferation; Differentiate to the right cell type"]]},{"id":27006,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"What distinguishes intercellular from intracellular signalling?","o":["Inter: cell to cell; intra: within the cell","Inter: within the cell; intra: cell to cell","Inter: via blood only; intra: via junctions only","Inter: gases only; intra: hormones only","They are identical"],"e":"<b>Intercellular</b> (extracellular): cell to cell (Part I). <b>Intracellular</b>: within the cell (Part II). Memory aid: inter = between (international); intra = inside.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Different."],"k":"Inter vs intra.","rn":[["","What is intracellular signalling?","What is intracellular signalling?","Signal sent within the cell; Changes an activity of another or the same part of the cell"]]},{"id":27007,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which are the three types of intercellular signal?","o":["Chemical, physical and gaseous","Chemical, electrical and thermal (via junctions)","Hormonal, neuronal and gaseous","Chemical, physical and magnetic","Light, sound and touch"],"e":"<b>Chemical</b> (majority; short range e.g. neurotransmitters, long range e.g. hormones); <b>physical</b> (tight, adherens, desmosome, gap junctions, hemidesmosome); <b>gaseous</b> (O₂, NO, CO).","w":["Physical (junctions) and gaseous, with chemical the majority.","Hormones/neurons are chemical.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Chemical, physical, gaseous.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of intercellular signals?","What are the 3 types of intercellular signals?","Chemical (majority); Physical; Gaseous"]]},{"id":27008,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Name the junction that anchors a cell's intermediate filaments to the basal lamina.","e":"Hemidesmosomes anchor to the basal lamina; desmosomes join cells; adherens junctions join actin bundles; tight junctions seal.","k":"Hemidesmosome.","a":["hemidesmosome","hemidesmosomes"],"rn":[["","Hemidesmosome","Hemidesmosome","Cell Junctions that anchor intermediate filaments in a cell to the basal lamina."]]},{"id":27009,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Name the junction that joins actin bundles of neighbouring cells.","e":"Adherens junctions link actin bundles.","k":"Adherens junction.","a":["adherens junction","adherens junctions","adherens","zonula adherens"],"rn":[["","What is Adherens junction?","What is Adherens junction?","Cell Junctions that join an actin bundle in one cell to a similar bundle in a neighbouring cell"]]},{"id":27010,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Name the junction that prevents leakage of molecules between epithelial cells.","e":"Tight junctions seal the space between epithelial cells.","k":"Tight junction.","a":["tight junction","tight junctions","zonula occludens","occluding junction"],"rn":[["","What is Tight Junctions?","What is Tight Junctions?","Cell Junctions that seal neighbouring cells together in an epithelial sheet to prevent leakage of molecules between them."]]},{"id":27011,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","f":"cs13","s":"Which sequence describes chemical intercellular signalling?","o":["Release → transport → receptor → response → removal","Release → receptor → transport → response → removal","Transport → release → receptor → removal → response","Receptor → release → transport → response → removal","Release → transport → removal → receptor → response"],"e":"Five steps: <b>synthesis and release</b>; <b>transport</b>; <b>detection</b> by a specific receptor; <b>change in cellular behaviour</b> (via intracellular signalling); <b>removal</b> of the signal (sometimes of the receptor). Memory aid: “Some Teenagers Download Cool Ringtones”.","w":["Transport before detection.","Release first.","Detection after transport.","Removal last."],"k":"Five steps of signalling.","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps of chemical intercellular signalling?","What are the 5 steps of chemical intercellular signalling?","Synthesis and release; Transport to target cell; Detection by a specific receptor; Change in cellular behaviour; Removal of the signal"]]},{"id":27012,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Why must signalling be switched off (step 5)?","o":["Constant signalling would be too much","Signals never stop once started, so cells must break down receptors","To make new receptors for the next signal","To cause apoptosis every time","To make DNA"],"e":"Can you keep signalling constant? No – like asking someone to stop on repeat. Communication must <b>shut down</b> so the body resets. Removal may be of the signal or receptor.","w":["Communication must shut down so the body can reset.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Signal termination resets.","rn":[["","Why can't most signalling molecules cross the cell membrane?","Why can't most signalling molecules cross the cell membrane?","Too big or charged; Lipid bilayer structure"]]},{"id":27013,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Why do most signalling molecules act on the cell surface rather than entering?","o":["They are too big or charged to cross the bilayer","They are too small and uncharged to be held inside","They are hydrophobic and dissolve in the membrane","The membrane is freely permeable, so they pass back out","They are all gases"],"e":"<b>Most</b> chemical messengers are <b>too big or charged</b> to cross the membrane (lipid bilayer) – protective. So many compounds and drugs act on <b>surface</b> receptors; others use carriers.","w":["Big/charged.","Hydrophobic ones enter.","It is protective.","Not."],"k":"Big/charged → surface receptors.","rn":[["","Why can't most signalling molecules cross the cell membrane?","Why can't most signalling molecules cross the cell membrane?","Too big or charged; Lipid bilayer structure"]]},{"id":27014,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which of the following is listed as a gaseous signalling molecule?","o":["Hydrogen sulphide (H₂S)","Histamine (released from mast cells)","Glucagon","Insulin","Cortisol"],"e":"Messengers: proteins (insulin), peptides (glucagon, growth hormone), sugars, lipid-derived, hormones, cytokines, neurotransmitters, pheromones, gases (<b>NO, H₂S</b>). Not exhaustive.","w":["Histamine is an amino acid derivative.","Peptide.","Protein.","Steroid."],"k":"Gases: NO, H₂S."},{"id":27015,"t":"Intracellular Signalling and Its Control","s":"How do some toxins enter cells to cause damage?","o":["They exploit transporters, e.g. for amino acids","They exploit gap junctions between neighbouring cells","They dissolve the nucleus","They bind DNA outside the cell","They never enter"],"e":"Some very potent compounds (toxins) take advantage of transporters (e.g. for amino acids) to enter cells.","w":["Transporters.","Not.","Not.","They do."],"k":"Toxins exploit transporters.","rn":[["","What are the 3 most common amino acids phosphorylated?","What are the 3 most common amino acids phosphorylated?","Serine; Threonine; Tyrosine"],["","What defines the specificity of an ion channel?","What defines the specificity of an ion channel?","Amino acids lining the channel"],["","What are the 3 types of paracrine signals?","What are the 3 types of paracrine signals?","Proteins (e.g. cytokines, platelet-derived growth factor); Amino acid derivatives (e.g. histamine); Dissolved gas (e.g. nitric oxide)"]]},{"id":27016,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which definition of an antagonist is correct?","o":["A molecule that binds and inactivates a receptor","A molecule that binds and activates a receptor","Any molecule that binds a receptor","A receptor protein","An enzyme"],"e":"<b>Ligand</b>: binds a receptor. <b>Agonist</b>: binds and activates (usual effect). <b>Antagonist/blocker</b>: binds and inactivates (stops the action).","w":["Agonist.","Ligand.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Antagonist inactivates.","rn":[["","What is a ligand?","What is a ligand?","A molecule that binds to a receptor"],["","First vs second messenger?","First vs second messenger?","First: molecule binding the receptor; Second: intracellular molecule changed by it"],["","What are 2 names for molecules that bind to and inactivate receptors?","What are 2 names for molecules that bind to and inactivate receptors?","Antagonists; Blockers"]]},{"id":27017,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Why don't all cells react to the same chemical at once?","o":["Different cells express different receptors","All cells have every receptor, but most are switched off","Chemicals cannot travel far enough","Receptors are identical everywhere","Cells lack membranes"],"e":"Cells respond via <b>specific</b> receptors; <b>different cells express different receptors</b>. Exposure can modify receptor expression/activity.","w":["Not all expressed.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Different cells, different receptors.","rn":[["","Do different cells have the same receptors?","Do different cells have the same receptors?","No, different cells express different receptors; Exposure to signals can change signal expression or activity"]]},{"id":27018,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"What does it mean that receptors are “specific”?","o":["Activated by certain ligands, not all","Activated by one ligand only, ever","Activated by any molecule nearby","They are lipids","They never change"],"e":"“Specific” doesn't mean one ligand only; they can be activated by different ones but <b>not by all</b> – relatively specific.","w":["Relatively specific.","Not.","Proteins.","Exposure modifies them."],"k":"Relatively specific.","rn":[["","Does an \"inhibitory\" G protein mean the ligand inhibits the receptor?","Does an \"inhibitory\" G protein mean the ligand inhibits the receptor?","No; An agonist activates an inhibitory pathway"]]},{"id":27019,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which are the three classes of transmembrane receptor?","o":["GPCRs, ion channel-linked and enzyme-linked","GPCRs, ion channel-linked and nuclear receptors","Nuclear, cytoplasmic and mitochondrial","Gap junctions, desmosomes and tight junctions","Ligands, agonists and antagonists"],"e":"Transmembrane/cell surface: <b>GPCRs</b>, <b>ion channel linked</b>, <b>enzyme linked</b>. Plus cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors. Transmembrane are the majority.","w":["Nuclear is intracellular.","Not.","Junctions.","Molecules."],"k":"Three transmembrane classes.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of transmembrane receptors?","What are the 3 types of transmembrane receptors?","G protein-coupled receptors; Ion channel linked receptors; Enzyme linked receptors"]]},{"id":27020,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","f":"cs17","s":"How does a cell-surface receptor transmit a signal without the ligand entering the cell?","o":["Binding changes the receptor’s intracellular domains","The ligand always enters the cell with the receptor","The receptor dissolves into the cytoplasm","It opens the nucleus","It uses gap junctions"],"e":"Agonist binding changes the <b>intracellular domains</b>' activities, initiating the response; the ligand doesn't need to enter.","w":["It need not enter.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Surface receptors signal inward.","rn":[["","Does a signalling molecule need to enter the cell to act on a cell-surface receptor?","Does a signalling molecule need to enter the cell to act on a cell-surface receptor?","No; Agonist binding changes the receptor's intracellular domains, starting the response"]]},{"id":27021,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","f":"cs18","s":"Which molecules can use cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","o":["Small (NO) and hydrophobic (steroid) molecules","Large charged proteins such as insulin and growth hormone","Peptides such as glucagon","Neurotransmitters such as ACh","Histamine"],"e":"<b>Small</b> molecules (nitric oxide) and <b>hydrophobic</b> molecules (steroid hormones, thyroxin) cross the membrane and bind receptors in the cytosol or nucleus.","w":["These need surface receptors.","Surface.","Surface.","Surface."],"k":"Small/hydrophobic → internal receptors.","rn":[["","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Small molecules (e.g. nitric oxide); Hydrophobic molecules (e.g. steroid hormones, thyroxin)"]]},{"id":27022,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"How do cortisol and corticosteroid drugs act at the cellular level?","o":["They enter and bind a receptor that goes to the nucleus","They bind surface ion channels and change membrane potential","They act via gap junctions","They block GPCRs","They degrade mRNA"],"e":"Cortisol/corticosteroids cross the membrane, bind the receptor (often cytoplasmic), and the receptor moves to the nucleus to change transcription. How it travels is still partly unknown.","w":["They act on intracellular receptors and gene transcription.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Steroids → nuclear transcription.","rn":[["","Does a signalling molecule need to enter the cell to act on a cell-surface receptor?","Does a signalling molecule need to enter the cell to act on a cell-surface receptor?","No; Agonist binding changes the receptor's intracellular domains, starting the response"]]},{"id":27023,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","f":"cs19","s":"Which ordering of receptor response speed is correct?","o":["Ion channel (ms) > GPCR (s) > kinase ≈ nuclear (h)","GPCR (ms) > ion channel (s) > kinase ≈ nuclear (h)","Ion channel (ms) > kinase (s) > GPCR ≈ nuclear (h)","Nuclear (ms) > kinase (s) > GPCR ≈ ion channel (h)","All equal"],"e":"Rang & Dale: <b>ion channels</b> (ionotropic) – <b>milliseconds</b> (nicotinic ACh); <b>GPCRs</b> (metabotropic) – <b>seconds</b> (muscarinic ACh); <b>kinase-linked</b> – <b>hours</b> (cytokine receptors); <b>nuclear</b> – <b>hours</b> (oestrogen receptor).","w":["Channels fastest.","GPCR seconds.","Reverse.","Not."],"k":"Receptor speed order.","rn":[["","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","Ligand-gated ion channels; G protein-coupled receptors; Kinase-linked receptors; Nuclear receptors"],["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"]]},{"id":27024,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"The nicotinic acetylcholine receptor responds within milliseconds. Name its receptor class.","e":"Ion channel (ms) > GPCR (s) > kinase-linked ≈ nuclear (hours).","k":"Ligand-gated ion channel.","a":["ligand-gated ion channel","ligand gated ion channel","ion channel","ionotropic","ion channel-linked receptor","ion channel linked"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"],["","Ligand-gated ion channels are classified according to what?","Ligand-gated ion channels are classified according to what?","The ligand binding to them"],["","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","Ligand-gated ion channels; G protein-coupled receptors; Kinase-linked receptors; Nuclear receptors"]]},{"id":27025,"t":"Enzyme-Linked and Nuclear Receptors","s":"An oestrogen receptor response is expected to take:","o":["Hours","Milliseconds","Seconds","Minutes","Instantly"],"e":"Nuclear receptors (e.g. oestrogen): gene transcription → hours.","w":["Channels.","GPCRs.","Not given.","Not."],"k":"Nuclear: hours.","rn":[["","How fast are RTK responses?","How fast are RTK responses?","Slow (hours)"]]},{"id":27026,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A cell receives no survival signals. Name the process it undergoes.","e":"Without survival signals, cells undergo apoptosis; division needs additional signals.","k":"Apoptosis.","a":["apoptosis","programmed cell death"],"rn":[["","What happens to a cell deprived of survival signals?","What happens to a cell deprived of survival signals?","Apoptosis"]]},{"id":27027,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"What explains why different cells respond differently to the same signal?","o":["Its receptor set and intracellular machinery","The signal changing shape in each tissue","All cells having identical receptors","Signals never reaching some cells","Random chance"],"e":"Depends on <b>sets of receptors</b> and <b>intracellular machinery</b> – explaining unwanted drug effects.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Receptors + machinery.","rn":[["","What 2 things decide how a cell responds to a signal?","What 2 things decide how a cell responds to a signal?","The set of receptors it has; Its intracellular machinery"]]},{"id":27028,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Adrenaline contracts smooth muscle in vessels supplying the gut but relaxes vessels supplying muscles. Why?","o":["Gut vessels: alpha (contract); muscle vessels: beta (relax)","Gut vessels: beta (contract); muscle vessels: alpha (relax)","Gut vessels: alpha (relax); muscle vessels: beta (contract)","Adrenaline differs in each tissue","Gut vessels lack receptors"],"e":"<b>Alpha</b> receptors → contraction (gut vessels); <b>beta</b> → relaxation (muscle vessels). Same compound, different receptor → different effect.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped effects.","Same molecule.","Not."],"k":"Alpha vs beta adrenoceptors.","rn":[["","Adrenaline: effect on smooth muscle of vessels supplying the muscles?","Adrenaline: effect on smooth muscle of vessels supplying the muscles?","Relaxation (beta receptors)"],["","Adrenaline: effect on smooth muscle of gut blood vessels?","Adrenaline: effect on smooth muscle of gut blood vessels?","Contraction (alpha receptors)"]]},{"id":27029,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"An asthmatic using salbutamol notices palpitations. What explains this?","o":["It dilates airways via β2 but can raise heart rate via β1","It dilates airways via β1 but can raise heart rate via β2","It blocks alpha receptors in the heart","It is an allergic reaction","It acts on nicotinic receptors"],"e":"<b>Salbutamol</b>: binds some adrenaline receptors; dilates airways (<b>beta-2</b>), can cause increased heart rate (<b>beta-1</b>) – unwanted effect. Same GPCR mechanism, different tissue.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Salbutamol side effect via β1.","rn":[["","Salbutamol: which receptor dilates airways?","Salbutamol: which receptor dilates airways?","Beta-2"],["","Salbutamol: which receptor causes increased heart rate?","Salbutamol: which receptor causes increased heart rate?","Beta-1"]]},{"id":27030,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","f":"cs22","s":"Acetylcholine causes secretion in salivary glands, slows heart pacemaker cells and contracts skeletal muscle. Which receptors mediate each?","o":["Saliva: M1 (GPCR); heart: M2 (GPCR); skeletal: nicotinic","Saliva: M2 (GPCR); heart: M1 (GPCR); skeletal: nicotinic","Saliva: nicotinic; heart: M2 (GPCR); skeletal: M1 (GPCR)","All three nicotinic ion channels","All three M2 GPCRs"],"e":"ACh: <b>salivary</b> – secretion (M1 GPCR); <b>heart pacemaker</b> – decreased firing (M2 GPCR); <b>skeletal muscle</b> – contraction (<b>nicotinic</b>, Na⁺/K⁺ channel). Similar GPCRs can be interpreted differently; skeletal muscle uses a different receptor.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ACh: M1, M2, nicotinic.","rn":[["","How does acetylcholine act on a heart pacemaker cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a heart pacemaker cell?","Binds M2 receptor (GPCR, a G protein-coupled receptor); Decreases rate of firing"],["","The nicotinic receptor is important for what?","The nicotinic receptor is important for what?","Skeletal muscle contraction"],["","How does acetylcholine act on a skeletal muscle cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a skeletal muscle cell?","Binds nicotinic-type receptor (Na+/K+ channel); Causes contraction"]]},{"id":27031,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Why does ACh produce different effects in salivary gland and heart cells despite both using GPCRs?","o":["Each cell interprets the signal differently","They use different ligands that both happen to bind GPCRs","One cell type lacks receptors","ACh changes structure","They use gap junctions"],"e":"Similar receptor proteins (GPCRs), but signals are <b>interpreted differently</b> in specialised cells.","w":["The ligand is the same (ACh).","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Interpretation differs by cell.","rn":[["","Why do drugs sometimes have unwanted effects?","Why do drugs sometimes have unwanted effects?","Different cells respond differently to the same signal"]]},{"id":27032,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Insulin travels in the blood from the pancreas to muscle and fat. Name this long-range signalling mode.","e":"Endocrine: hormones in blood to distant targets; slow.","k":"Endocrine.","a":["endocrine","endocrine signalling","endocrine signaling"],"rn":[["","Which is the only long range mode of signalling?","Which is the only long range mode of signalling?","Endocrine"]]},{"id":27033,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"What defines a hormone?","o":["Made by an endocrine gland, carried in blood to distant targets","Made by any cell, diffusing locally to neighbouring targets","Made by a neuron, crossing a synapse to one target","Made by an endocrine gland, acting on the cell that made it","A membrane-bound molecule"],"e":"<b>Hormone</b>: produced by an <b>endocrine gland</b>, released into the <b>bloodstream</b>, acts at a distance. Only cells with the correct receptor respond. Hormones affect gene expression.","w":["Paracrine.","Neuronal.","Autocrine.","Juxtacrine."],"k":"Hormone definition.","rn":[["","What is Hormone?","What is Hormone?","Produced by an endocrine gland; Released into the bloodstream; Acts on target cells at a distant location"]]},{"id":27034,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Why is endocrine signalling described as slow?","o":["Hormones travel in blood; effects need genes","Hormones never reach their targets without a carrier protein in blood","Blood moves slowly in all vessels","It uses gap junctions","It needs synapses"],"e":"Slow: release, travel in the bloodstream, and effects (gene expression); not immediate cell-to-cell contact.","w":["They reach targets; it is slow.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Endocrine slow.","rn":[["","Why is endocrine signalling long range?","Why is endocrine signalling long range?","Hormones circulate in the blood; Reach most cells in the body"],["","How do hormones regulate cell reactions?","How do hormones regulate cell reactions?","By affecting gene expression"],["","Which receptor type comes to mind for hormones that affect gene expression?","Which receptor type comes to mind for hormones that affect gene expression?","Nuclear (cytoplasmic) receptors"]]},{"id":27035,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Can adrenaline be both a hormone and a neurotransmitter?","o":["Yes – depending on how it is made","No – it is only a hormone, released from the adrenal gland","No – only a neurotransmitter","Only in bacteria","Yes – but only in the gut"],"e":"Adrenaline can be a neurotransmitter or a hormone depending on how it is synthesised.","w":["It can be either.","Both.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Adrenaline: both roles.","rn":[["","Adrenaline can be which 2 things, depending on how it's made?","Adrenaline can be which 2 things, depending on how it's made?","Neurotransmitter; Hormone"]]},{"id":27036,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"What is the signalling error in type 2 diabetes?","o":["Decreased response to insulin","No insulin is made by the pancreas","Too much glucagon","Gap junction failure","Cortisol deficiency"],"e":"<b>Type 1</b>: no insulin made (no signal). <b>Type 2</b>: absent/decreased <b>response</b> to insulin. Insulin is secreted by the pancreas and acts at insulin receptors to promote glucose absorption.","w":["That is type 1.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"T2DM = reduced response.","rn":[["","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1: no insulin made; Type 2: absent or decreased response to insulin"]]},{"id":27037,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"A child with type 1 diabetes has which signalling error?","o":["No insulin is made","Insulin receptors are resistant","Too many insulin receptors","Cortisol excess","Nitric oxide excess"],"e":"<b>Type 1 diabetes</b>: no insulin made.","w":["Type 2.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"T1DM = no signal.","rn":[["","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1: no insulin made; Type 2: absent or decreased response to insulin"]]},{"id":27038,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about cortisol and the glucocorticoid receptor is correct?","o":["GR activates and suppresses genes","GR only activates genes, giving metabolic effects","Cortisol binds an ion channel","GR is only on the surface","Cortisol is a paracrine gas"],"e":"<b>Cortisol</b> binds the <b>glucocorticoid receptor (GR)</b>, which <b>both activates and suppresses</b> gene expression – metabolic and anti-inflammatory effects.","w":["It both activates and suppresses.","Not.","Intracellular.","Not."],"k":"Cortisol–GR effects.","rn":[["","What happens in the cell after a nuclear receptor is activated?","What happens in the cell after a nuclear receptor is activated?","Gene transcription; Protein synthesis"],["","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","Protein phosphorylation; Gene transcription, then protein synthesis"],["","What are 3 examples of responses?","What are 3 examples of responses?","Turning on a gene; Activating an enzyme; Rearranging the cytoskeleton"]]},{"id":27039,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Corticosteroid drugs are used in which conditions?","o":["Autoimmunity, allergy and asthma","Type 1 diabetes, allergy and asthma","Angina and heart block","Bacterial infections only","Autoimmunity and angina"],"e":"Corticosteroids: synthetic GR activators used in <b>autoimmunity, allergic reactions, asthma</b>.","w":["Insulin.","GTN.","Not.","GTN."],"k":"Corticosteroid uses.","rn":[["","What are 3 clinical uses of corticosteroids?","What are 3 clinical uses of corticosteroids?","Autoimmunity; Allergic reactions; Asthma"]]},{"id":27040,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which receptor types do hormones use?","o":["Nuclear; she added tyrosine kinase receptors too","Ion channels only; she added gap junctions","GPCRs only; she added nothing","Nuclear only; insulin also acts on a nuclear receptor","No receptors"],"e":"Answer: <b>nuclear</b> (gene transcription); she added <b>tyrosine kinase receptors</b> can too; insulin acts on a <b>transmembrane</b> receptor.","w":["Not.","Not.","Insulin is transmembrane.","Not."],"k":"Hormone receptor types.","rn":[["","What does RTK stand for?","What does RTK stand for?","Receptor tyrosine kinase"],["","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","Ligand-gated ion channels; G protein-coupled receptors; Kinase-linked receptors; Nuclear receptors"],["","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Which enzyme is usually part of an enzyme linked receptor?","Protein tyrosine kinase"]]},{"id":27041,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","f":"cs27","s":"Which statement describes paracrine signalling?","o":["Released and diffuse locally to neighbours","Released into blood to distant targets","Released and act on the releasing cell","Membrane-bound, needing contact","Released across a synapse only"],"e":"<b>Paracrine</b>: released and <b>diffuse locally</b> to neighbours. Signals: proteins (cytokines, PDGF), amino acid derivatives (histamine), dissolved gas (NO).","w":["Endocrine.","Autocrine.","Juxtacrine.","Neuronal."],"k":"Paracrine = local diffusion.","rn":[["","What is paracrine signalling?","What is paracrine signalling?","Signalling molecules released from one cell; Diffuse locally to neighbouring cells"]]},{"id":27042,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which pairing of paracrine signal and class is correct?","o":["Histamine – amino acid derivative","Nitric oxide – protein","Cytokines – dissolved gas","PDGF – amino acid derivative","Histamine – protein"],"e":"Proteins (cytokines, PDGF); amino acid derivatives (<b>histamine</b>); dissolved gas (<b>NO</b>).","w":["Gas.","Proteins.","Protein.","Derivative."],"k":"Histamine = amino acid derivative.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of paracrine signals?","What are the 3 types of paracrine signals?","Proteins (e.g. cytokines, platelet-derived growth factor); Amino acid derivatives (e.g. histamine); Dissolved gas (e.g. nitric oxide)"]]},{"id":27043,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Nitric oxide diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates which enzyme to cause relaxation?","e":"NO (made by NOS) activates guanylate cyclase in smooth muscle; GTN mimics NO in angina.","k":"Guanylate cyclase.","a":["guanylate cyclase","guanylyl cyclase","soluble guanylate cyclase","sgc"],"rn":[["","What is the target enzyme of NO in smooth muscle cells?","What is the target enzyme of NO in smooth muscle cells?","Guanylate cyclase"]]},{"id":27044,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"A patient with angina is given GTN. What is its mechanism?","o":["A nitrate mimicking NO to dilate coronary arteries","A nitrate blocking NO to constrict coronary arteries","A beta-blocker","An insulin analogue","A corticosteroid"],"e":"<b>Medicinal nitrates</b> (glyceryl trinitrate, <b>GTN</b>) are used in <b>angina</b> to dilate coronary arteries, mimicking NO.","w":["Mimics.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"GTN mimics NO.","rn":[["","Which medicinal nitrate is used in angina, and how does it help?","Which medicinal nitrate is used in angina, and how does it help?","Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN); Dilates coronary arteries"]]},{"id":27045,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","f":"cs29","s":"Why is neuronal signalling classed as short range, despite long nerves?","o":["The transmitter only crosses the synapse","The nerves themselves are always short, a few millimetres","It uses the blood like hormones","It uses gap junctions","It has no molecule"],"e":"Some authors call it long range (nerve length), but classification is <b>how far the molecule travels</b>: neurotransmitters cross only the <b>synaptic gap</b> – short range.","w":["Nerves can be long; the molecule travels a short way.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Neuronal: short (synapse)."},{"id":27046,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which is an example of neuronal signalling?","o":["Noradrenaline at adrenoceptors contracting vessels","Noradrenaline at nicotinic receptors relaxing vessels","Insulin acting on the liver","Interleukin-1 acting on monocytes","NO acting on smooth muscle"],"e":"Example: <b>noradrenaline</b>, from a <b>sympathetic nerve terminal</b>, acts at <b>adrenoceptors</b> to <b>contract blood vessels</b>.","w":["Adrenoceptors; contract.","Endocrine.","Autocrine.","Paracrine."],"k":"Noradrenaline neuronal example.","rn":[["","What does noradrenaline do at adrenoceptors on blood vessels?","What does noradrenaline do at adrenoceptors on blood vessels?","Contracts blood vessels"]]},{"id":27047,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Interleukin-1 produced by a monocyte binds receptors on the same monocyte. Name this signalling mode.","e":"Autocrine signalling acts on the cell that made the signal; it can raise or lower its own production.","k":"Autocrine.","a":["autocrine","autocrine signalling","autocrine signaling"],"rn":[["","Image card: What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","Autocrine; Cell's molecules bind its own receptors"],["","What is the autocrine example, interleukin-1, produced in response to?","What is the autocrine example, interleukin-1, produced in response to?","External stimuli; It binds receptors on the same cell that made it"],["","What is autocrine signalling?","What is autocrine signalling?","Cell secretes molecules that bind its own receptors; Changes its own behaviour"]]},{"id":27048,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"How can autocrine signalling regulate a cell's own production of a molecule?","o":["It can raise or lower production","It always raises production, as positive feedback only","It always stops production","It has no feedback effect","It only affects neighbours"],"e":"Sometimes binding to own receptors increases (positive) or decreases (negative feedback) production.","w":["Positive or negative feedback.","Either.","Feedback.","Paracrine."],"k":"Autocrine feedback."},{"id":27049,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"What defines juxtacrine signalling?","o":["Contact between neighbours via membrane-bound molecules","Diffusion of released molecules to nearby neighbours","Hormones carried in the blood","Self-signalling","Neurotransmitter release"],"e":"<b>Juxtacrine</b>: contact-dependent; membrane-bound molecules; <b>doesn't require release</b>. Examples: receptor–ligand binding (antigen presentation, ECM interactions), <b>gap junctions</b>. Analogy: a kiss.","w":["Paracrine.","Endocrine.","Autocrine.","Neuronal."],"k":"Juxtacrine = contact.","rn":[["","What is juxtacrine signalling?","What is juxtacrine signalling?","Contact-dependent signalling; Immediate neighbours signal via membrane bound molecules"]]},{"id":27050,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which immune process is an example of juxtacrine signalling?","o":["Antigen presentation","Fever via cytokines in blood","Insulin release","Noradrenaline at synapses","NO diffusion"],"e":"Antigen presentation requires contact between two cell types (more in Immunology).","w":["Endocrine.","Endocrine.","Neuronal.","Paracrine."],"k":"Antigen presentation = juxtacrine.","rn":[["","Which immune process needs contact between two cell types?","Which immune process needs contact between two cell types?","Antigen presentation"]]},{"id":27051,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Name the proteins that form gap junction pores.","e":"Gap junctions are 2–4 nm connexin pores passing ions, nucleotides and sugars; essential in heart muscle.","k":"Connexins.","a":["connexins","connexin","connexons","connexon"],"rn":[["","What proteins form the gap junction pore?","What proteins form the gap junction pore?","Connexins"]]},{"id":27052,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Why are gap junctions essential in heart muscle?","o":["They pass excitation quickly between myocytes","They block contraction between myocytes to prevent arrhythmia","They secrete hormones","They anchor to basal lamina","They form tight seals"],"e":"Gap junctions between heart muscle cells allow <b>waves of electrical excitation</b> to pass quickly; ions like Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ change membrane potential.","w":["They pass the electrical wave.","Not.","Hemidesmosome.","Tight junction."],"k":"Gap junctions → cardiac coupling.","rn":[["","What do gap junctions do between heart muscle cells?","What do gap junctions do between heart muscle cells?","Let waves of electrical excitation pass quickly"]]},{"id":27053,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"What happens to uterine gap junctions towards the end of pregnancy?","o":["They increase near term, then fall after birth","They disappear before labour to relax the uterus","They never change","They form only after birth","They block contractions"],"e":"Gap junctions appear in the myometrium near term to coordinate uterine contractions; expression increases in labour, then decreases.","w":["Increase.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Uterine gap junctions in labour."},{"id":27054,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Is a gap junction chemical or physical communication?","o":["Juxtacrine, but actually physical","Endocrine, but actually chemical","Purely chemical with receptors","Gaseous","Neuronal"],"e":"A gap junction is juxtacrine (contact) but actually <b>physical</b> communication – a pore letting ions pass without ligand-gated or voltage-gated channels.","w":["Not.","No receptors.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gap junction = physical."},{"id":27056,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A drug activates the same receptor subtype in two tissues but causes a side effect in one. What best explains it?","o":["The receptor is in both tissues","The drug changes structure in one tissue","Side effects are random","Receptors are absent elsewhere","Drugs create new receptors"],"e":"Understanding where signals act explains side effects; same mechanism in different tissues.","w":["Not.","Not.","Present.","Not."],"k":"Side effects from receptor distribution.","rn":[["","Do different cells have the same receptors?","Do different cells have the same receptors?","No, different cells express different receptors; Exposure to signals can change signal expression or activity"]]},{"id":27057,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Histamine released by a mast cell acts on nearby blood vessels. Name this signalling mode.","e":"Paracrine: local diffusion to neighbours (histamine, NO, PDGF).","k":"Paracrine.","a":["paracrine","paracrine signalling","paracrine signaling"],"rn":[["","Image card: What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","Paracrine; Local mediator diffuses to neighbouring target cells"],["","What is paracrine signalling?","What is paracrine signalling?","Signalling molecules released from one cell; Diffuse locally to neighbouring cells"],["","What are the 5 modes of intercellular signalling?","What are the 5 modes of intercellular signalling?","Endocrine; Paracrine; Neuronal; Autocrine; Juxtacrine; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":27058,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Insulin released by the pancreas acts on muscle and fat. Which mode is this?","o":["Endocrine","Paracrine","Autocrine","Juxtacrine","Neuronal"],"e":"Insulin is a hormone from the pancreas released into the blood – endocrine.","w":["Local.","Self.","Contact.","Synapse."],"k":"Insulin endocrine.","rn":[["","Which is the only long range mode of signalling?","Which is the only long range mode of signalling?","Endocrine"],["","What is Hormone?","What is Hormone?","Produced by an endocrine gland; Released into the bloodstream; Acts on target cells at a distant location"]]},{"id":27059,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about oxygen as a signal is correct?","o":["Gases such as O₂, NO and CO signal","Oxygen has no signalling role","Oxygen is a protein signal","Oxygen acts only on nuclear receptors","Oxygen is a hormone"],"e":"Gaseous signals: oxygen, nitric oxide, carbon monoxide.","w":["It does.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gases signal.","rn":[["","What are 3 examples of gaseous signals?","What are 3 examples of gaseous signals?","Oxygen; Nitric oxide; Carbon monoxide"]]},{"id":27061,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A hydrophilic peptide hormone and a lipophilic steroid hormone both reach a cell. How do their receptor locations differ?","o":["Peptide: surface receptor; steroid: intracellular receptor","Peptide: intracellular receptor; steroid: surface receptor","Both use nuclear receptors","Both use gap junctions","The peptide enters the nucleus"],"e":"Hydrophilic/large → surface receptors; small/hydrophobic (steroids) → intracellular receptors.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Peptide surface; steroid internal.","rn":[["","Does a signalling molecule need to enter the cell to act on a cell-surface receptor?","Does a signalling molecule need to enter the cell to act on a cell-surface receptor?","No; Agonist binding changes the receptor's intracellular domains, starting the response"],["","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Small molecules (e.g. nitric oxide); Hydrophobic molecules (e.g. steroid hormones, thyroxin)"],["","What are 4 examples of nuclear receptors?","What are 4 examples of nuclear receptors?","Oestrogen; Thyroid; Retinoic acid; Steroid hormone receptors"]]},{"id":27062,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which receptor class would mediate a response within milliseconds?","o":["Ligand-gated ion channel","GPCR","Kinase-linked receptor","Nuclear receptor","Glucocorticoid receptor"],"e":"Ion channels: milliseconds.","w":["Seconds.","Hours.","Hours.","Hours."],"k":"Fastest: ion channels.","rn":[["","Ligand-gated ion channels are classified according to what?","Ligand-gated ion channels are classified according to what?","The ligand binding to them"],["","What is a ligand-gated ion channel (ionotropic receptor)?","What is a ligand-gated ion channel (ionotropic receptor)?","Transmembrane protein: receptor part plus channel; Opens when a ligand binds"],["","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","What are the 2 types of acetylcholine receptor?","Nicotinic (ligand-gated ion channel); Muscarinic (GPCR)"]]},{"id":27063,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Cytokine receptors belong to which class, with what time scale?","o":["Kinase-linked – hours","Kinase-linked – seconds","GPCRs – seconds","Ion channels – milliseconds","Nuclear – milliseconds"],"e":"Kinase-linked (e.g. cytokine receptors): protein phosphorylation, gene transcription – hours.","w":["Hours.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Cytokines: kinase-linked.","rn":[["","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","Protein phosphorylation; Gene transcription, then protein synthesis"]]},{"id":27064,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Muscarinic ACh receptors are which type and with what effects?","o":["GPCRs – second messengers, seconds","Ion channels – ion flow, milliseconds","GPCRs – gene transcription, hours","Nuclear – transcription, hours","Kinase-linked – phosphorylation, hours"],"e":"GPCRs (metabotropic): seconds; e.g. muscarinic ACh receptor.","w":["Nicotinic.","Seconds.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Muscarinic = GPCR.","rn":[["","How fast are GPCR responses?","How fast are GPCR responses?","Seconds"]]},{"id":27065,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which explains why many drugs act on surface receptors rather than inside cells?","o":["The membrane is protective","Intracellular receptors don’t exist for drugs to reach","Drugs are always gases","Surface receptors are always faster","Cells lack cytoplasm"],"e":"Membrane is super protective; “once you enter the cell you have access to everything”. Many drugs act on surface receptors; others use carriers.","w":["They exist; many compounds can’t cross.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Drugs mostly act on surface.","rn":[["","What do integral membrane receptor proteins do?","What do integral membrane receptor proteins do?","Span the membrane; Transmit the signal into the cell"],["","Cytoplasmatic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Cytoplasmatic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Signals that can cross the cell membrane"],["","Why is the inactivated state protective?","Why is the inactivated state protective?","Channel can't reopen straight away; Otherwise tachycardic all the time"]]},{"id":27066,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"What can repeated exposure to signals do to a cell?","o":["Modify receptor expression or activity","Destroy the DNA of cells that are overexposed","Always increase receptors","Remove membranes","Create new ligands"],"e":"<b>Exposure to signals can modify signal (receptor) expression or activity.</b>","w":["Not stated.","Either.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Exposure modifies receptors.","rn":[["","Do different cells have the same receptors?","Do different cells have the same receptors?","No, different cells express different receptors; Exposure to signals can change signal expression or activity"]]},{"id":27067,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which pairing of mode and example is correct?","o":["Juxtacrine – gap junctions; autocrine – IL-1; paracrine – NO","Juxtacrine – NO; autocrine – IL-1; paracrine – gap junctions","Juxtacrine – gap junctions; autocrine – NO; paracrine – IL-1","Endocrine – NO; paracrine – insulin","Neuronal – cortisol"],"e":"Gap junctions = juxtacrine; IL-1 monocytes = autocrine; NO = paracrine; insulin/cortisol = endocrine; noradrenaline = neuronal.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mode–example pairs.","rn":[["","What are the 5 modes of intercellular signalling?","What are the 5 modes of intercellular signalling?","Endocrine; Paracrine; Neuronal; Autocrine; Juxtacrine; MNEMONIC"],["","What are the 2 examples of juxtacrine signalling?","What are the 2 examples of juxtacrine signalling?","Contact dependent receptor ligand binding; Communication junctions: gap junctions"]]},{"id":27068,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"A cell has receptors for A, B and C but not ligand K. Ligand K is present. What happens?","o":["It doesn’t respond to K","It responds strongly to K","It dies","It divides","It differentiates"],"e":"A cell responds only to signals it has receptors for (she mentioned a ligand “K” without a receptor).","w":["No receptor.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"No receptor, no response.","rn":[["","What are the 7 reasons cells communicate?","What are the 7 reasons cells communicate?","Respond to immediate environment (incl. danger); Respond to central commands; Respond to local commands; Respond accordingly; Coordinate cellular responses; Integrate signals from multiple sources; Induce / decrease growth (division)"],["","What 2 things decide how a cell responds to a signal?","What 2 things decide how a cell responds to a signal?","The set of receptors it has; Its intracellular machinery"],["","Can one cell respond to a single signal in several ways at once?","Can one cell respond to a single signal in several ways at once?","Yes, some cells do"]]},{"id":27070,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which statement about stress and cortisol is correct?","o":["Not all stress is pathological","All stress is pathological, and cortisol is what causes the damage","Stress has no hormones","Cortisol is paracrine","Cortisol is always harmful"],"e":"Stress is not only pathological (e.g. studying for exams); cortisol is part of the response; “we wouldn't survive without cortisol”.","w":["Some stress is fine.","Not.","Endocrine.","Not."],"k":"Stress can be normal."},{"id":27071,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement summarises why the same ligand can have different effects?","o":["Different receptors, subtypes or tissues","Ligands mutate in each tissue they reach","Receptors are identical everywhere","Effects are random","Only the dose matters"],"e":"Recap: different receptors, subtypes, or tissues (and machinery).","w":["The ligand is the same.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Why same ligand differs.","rn":[["","Do different cells have the same receptors?","Do different cells have the same receptors?","No, different cells express different receptors; Exposure to signals can change signal expression or activity"]]},{"id":27072,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which statement about gap junction pore selectivity is correct?","o":["Tiny pores; connexins limit what passes","Large pores that let any protein pass between cells","They pass only water","They need ligands to open","They are voltage-gated channels"],"e":"Pores very small; connexins can limit what passes via attraction/repulsion; no ligand-gated/voltage-gated channel needed.","w":["Pores are 2–4 nm.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Gap junction selectivity.","rn":[["","What proteins form the gap junction pore?","What proteins form the gap junction pore?","Connexins"]]},{"id":27075,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"Which explanation of “interrupt” and “restore” communication describes how ibuprofen or paracetamol work conceptually?","o":["They bring signalling back to the right level","They create new pathways for the body to use","They bypass receptors","They destroy cells","They use gap junctions"],"e":"Drugs bring signalling back to the right threshold, e.g. high temperature.","w":["They use existing pathways.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Drugs restore levels."},{"id":27076,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which molecule is a protein signal given as an example?","o":["Insulin","Glucagon (peptide)","Nitric oxide","Cortisol","Histamine"],"e":"Proteins (e.g. insulin); peptides (glucagon, growth hormone).","w":["Peptide.","Gas.","Steroid.","Amino acid derivative."],"k":"Insulin = protein.","rn":[["","What are 9 classes of signalling molecules?","What are 9 classes of signalling molecules?","Proteins (e.g. insulin); Peptides (e.g. glucagon, growth hormone); Sugars; Lipid-derived molecules; Hormones; Cytokines; Neurotransmitters; Pheromones; Gaseous molecules (NO, H2S)"],["","What are 2 examples of RTKs?","What are 2 examples of RTKs?","Insulin receptors; Growth factor receptors"],["","Insulin: where is it secreted from?","Insulin: where is it secreted from?","Pancreas"]]},{"id":27078,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about receptor proteins being the majority is correct?","o":["Mainly proteins","Mainly lipids in the membrane bilayer","Mainly DNA in the nucleus","Mainly sugars","Gases"],"e":"Receptors: proteins (majority).","w":["Proteins are the majority.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Receptors = proteins.","rn":[["","What are most receptors made of?","What are most receptors made of?","Proteins"],["","What does GPCR stand for?","What does GPCR stand for?","G protein-coupled receptor"],["","What is the largest group of receptors?","What is the largest group of receptors?","G protein-coupled receptors"]]},{"id":27079,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"How does the classification of signalling modes by range apply to paracrine signals?","o":["Short range – to neighbouring cells","Long range – through the blood","Contact only","Self only","Synapse only"],"e":"Paracrine: short distance (“not right next to her, but not all over the room”).","w":["Endocrine.","Juxtacrine.","Autocrine.","Neuronal."],"k":"Paracrine short range.","rn":[["","Long vs short range signalling: distance scale?","Long vs short range signalling: distance scale?","Long: metres; Short: micrometres"],["","What are the 2 ranges of chemical signals?","What are the 2 ranges of chemical signals?","Short range (e.g. neurotransmitters); Long range (e.g. hormones)"],["","Image card: What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","What mode of signalling is this? How can you tell?","Paracrine; Local mediator diffuses to neighbouring target cells"]]},{"id":27080,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"What is meant by “responding accordingly” and its failure?","o":["Failure to respond properly is pathology","Cells always respond correctly to central commands","Failure is normal","Responses are random","Only drugs respond"],"e":"We respond to central and local commands “hopefully accordingly”; when we don't, there is a pathology.","w":["When they don’t, there is pathology.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Inappropriate response = pathology."},{"id":27081,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A drug binds a receptor and blocks the natural ligand from acting. What is the drug called?","e":"Antagonist: binds and inactivates/blocks; agonist: activates like the natural ligand.","k":"Antagonist.","a":["antagonist","receptor antagonist","blocker"],"rn":[["","What are 2 names for molecules that bind to and inactivate receptors?","What are 2 names for molecules that bind to and inactivate receptors?","Antagonists; Blockers"]]},{"id":27082,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A drug binds a receptor and produces the same effect as the natural ligand. What is the drug called?","e":"Agonists mimic the natural ligand (e.g. salbutamol at β2).","k":"Agonist.","a":["agonist","receptor agonist"],"rn":[["","What is Agonist?","What is Agonist?","Binds to and activates a receptor"]]},{"id":27083,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Corticosteroid drugs relate to cortisol how?","o":["Synthetic GR activators mimicking cortisol","Synthetic GR blockers opposing cortisol","They block insulin","They are gases","They are neurotransmitters"],"e":"Corticosteroids: synthetic GR activators mimicking cortisol.","w":["Activators.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Corticosteroids mimic cortisol."},{"id":27084,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Why is stem cell signalling considered “key”?","o":["They must be told when to divide and what to be","They ignore signals and divide on a fixed schedule","They only respond to gases","They never divide","Signals are irrelevant"],"e":"“Good communication is KEY! Sending the right SIGNALS is KEY!”","w":["Signals direct them.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Right signals key.","rn":[["","How do extracellular signals work together to control a cell?","How do extracellular signals work together to control a cell?","Cells often need several signals in combination; Some signals survive, others make it grow, divide or differentiate; Some signals inhibit behaviours or induce apoptosis"]]},{"id":27085,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A drug binds the beta-2 adrenaline receptors in the airways and activates them. What type of molecule is it, and what effect is expected?","o":["An agonist – airway dilation","An antagonist – airway dilation","An agonist – airway constriction","An antagonist – airway constriction","A hormone – slowed heart rate"],"e":"An agonist binds and activates a receptor (the usual effect). Salbutamol binds adrenaline receptors: it dilates the airways via beta-2, but can increase heart rate via beta-1.","w":["An antagonist inactivates.","Beta-2 dilates airways.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Signalling reading."},{"id":27086,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which statement about how nuclear receptors reach the nucleus is correct?","o":["It is still a little unknown","It is fully understood and uses gap junctions","It never moves","It uses gap junctions","It moves via the blood"],"e":"How it travels is still a little unknown – theories exist.","w":["Still a little unknown.","It moves.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nuclear transport partly unknown."},{"id":27087,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about short- vs long-range chemical signals is correct?","o":["Neurotransmitters short; hormones long","Neurotransmitters long; hormones short","Both long range","Both contact-dependent","Neither"],"e":"Chemical: short range (neurotransmitters), long range (hormones).","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Range examples.","rn":[["","What are the 2 ranges of chemical signals?","What are the 2 ranges of chemical signals?","Short range (e.g. neurotransmitters); Long range (e.g. hormones)"]]},{"id":27088,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"What happens to a hormone that travels in the blood past a cell lacking its receptor?","o":["Nothing – it doesn’t respond","It is activated anyway","The hormone is degraded instantly","It grows","It dies"],"e":"Travelling in the blood doesn't mean interacting with every cell – needs the correct receptor.","w":["Needs receptor.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Receptor needed."},{"id":27089,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which explains why ions passing through gap junctions cause rapid effects?","o":["Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ change membrane potential fast","Ions act as hormones once inside the next cell","Ions bind DNA","Ions are slow","Ions block contraction"],"e":"Ions are a really quick way of communicating; Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ change membrane potential.","w":["They change membrane potential.","Not.","Fast.","Not."],"k":"Ions → rapid coupling.","rn":[["","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","What are the 2 triggers for an ion channel opening?","Change in membrane potential (voltage-gated); Chemical binding (ligand-gated)"]]},{"id":27090,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which mode depends on molecules that may never be released?","o":["Juxtacrine","Endocrine","Paracrine","Autocrine","Neuronal"],"e":"Juxtacrine: membrane-bound molecules, no release needed.","w":["Released.","Released.","Released.","Released."],"k":"Juxtacrine no release.","rn":[["","Does juxtacrine signalling need release of a signalling molecule?","Does juxtacrine signalling need release of a signalling molecule?","No"]]},{"id":27091,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which pairing of hormone abbreviation and name is correct?","o":["ADH – antidiuretic hormone","ADH – adrenal hormone","TSH – testosterone","Oxytocin – insulin","Cortisol – NO"],"e":"Students gave: TSH, insulin, oxytocin, ADH (antidiuretic hormone), cortisol, progesterone.","w":["ADH = antidiuretic hormone.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hormone examples."},{"id":27092,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which effect is <b>not</b> listed for nitric oxide?","o":["Insulin secretion","Vasorelaxation","Platelet aggregation","Immune responses","(All of the others are listed)"],"e":"NO: vasorelaxation, platelet aggregation, immune responses.","w":["Listed.","Listed.","Listed.","Option 1 not."],"k":"NO effects.","rn":[["","How does acetylcholine act on a salivary gland cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a salivary gland cell?","Binds M1 receptor (GPCR, a G protein-coupled receptor); Causes secretion"],["","Insulin: where is it secreted from?","Insulin: where is it secreted from?","Pancreas"],["","What are 2 examples of RTKs?","What are 2 examples of RTKs?","Insulin receptors; Growth factor receptors"]]},{"id":27093,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"NO has a short half-life. What does this imply for its signalling mode?","o":["It acts locally (paracrine)","It travels long distances (endocrine)","It acts via gap junctions only","It acts on itself (autocrine)","It never acts"],"e":"Short half-life + diffusion to nearby smooth muscle → paracrine.","w":["Short-lived.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Short half-life → local.","rn":[["","What is paracrine signalling?","What is paracrine signalling?","Signalling molecules released from one cell; Diffuse locally to neighbouring cells"]]},{"id":27094,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which finding involving NO in cardiovascular disease is correct?","o":["Reduced NO in most CV disorders","Increased NO in all heart disease, causing low blood pressure","No change in heart disease","NO causes angina","NO blocks GTN"],"e":"<b>Reduced levels</b> in most cardiovascular disorders.","w":["Reduced.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"NO reduced in CVD.","rn":[["","What happens to NO levels in most cardiovascular disorders?","What happens to NO levels in most cardiovascular disorders?","Reduced"]]},{"id":27095,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"Which of the following is an example of a central command?","o":["A signal from the brain","A local paracrine signal","An autocrine IL-1 loop","A gap junction ion flux","A tight junction seal"],"e":"Cells respond to central commands (e.g. brain/endocrine) and local commands.","w":["Local.","Local.","Local.","Not."],"k":"Central command example.","rn":[["","What are the 7 reasons cells communicate?","What are the 7 reasons cells communicate?","Respond to immediate environment (incl. danger); Respond to central commands; Respond to local commands; Respond accordingly; Coordinate cellular responses; Integrate signals from multiple sources; Induce / decrease growth (division)"]]},{"id":27096,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"In type 1 diabetes, which step of chemical signalling fails?","o":["Synthesis and release of the signal","Detection by the target cell’s receptor","Removal of the signal","Transport in the blood","Change in cell behaviour after binding"],"e":"Type 1 diabetes: no insulin is made, so there is no signal (step 1, synthesis and release, fails). Type 2: absent or decreased response to insulin by target cells (detection/response fails).","w":["That is type 2 (decreased response).","Not.","Insulin travels normally if made.","The problem is no signal."],"k":"Session split.","rn":[["","What are the 5 steps of chemical intercellular signalling?","What are the 5 steps of chemical intercellular signalling?","Synthesis and release; Transport to target cell; Detection by a specific receptor; Change in cellular behaviour; Removal of the signal"]]},{"id":27097,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"Which of these would be most rapidly affected by a nicotinic receptor antagonist?","o":["Skeletal muscle contraction","Gene transcription by oestrogen","Cortisol anti-inflammatory effects","Insulin glucose uptake","Salivary secretion via M1"],"e":"Nicotinic receptors mediate skeletal muscle contraction (ms).","w":["Nuclear.","Nuclear.","Not.","Muscarinic."],"k":"Nicotinic → skeletal muscle.","rn":[["","The nicotinic receptor is important for what?","The nicotinic receptor is important for what?","Skeletal muscle contraction"],["","How does acetylcholine act on a skeletal muscle cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a skeletal muscle cell?","Binds nicotinic-type receptor (Na+/K+ channel); Causes contraction"]]},{"id":27098,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which drug effect would a muscarinic M2 agonist have on the heart?","o":["Decreased pacemaker firing","Increased pacemaker firing","Skeletal muscle contraction","Salivary secretion","Bronchodilation"],"e":"ACh at M2 on pacemaker cells decreases rate of firing.","w":["M2 slows.","Nicotinic.","M1.","Not."],"k":"M2 slows heart.","rn":[["","How does acetylcholine act on a heart pacemaker cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a heart pacemaker cell?","Binds M2 receptor (GPCR, a G protein-coupled receptor); Decreases rate of firing"]]},{"id":27099,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Interleukin-1 released by a monocyte binds receptors on that same monocyte. Which mode of signalling is this?","o":["Autocrine","Paracrine","Juxtacrine","Endocrine","Neuronal"],"e":"Autocrine signalling: a cell acts on itself (IL-1 in monocytes). Binding to its own receptors can increase (positive feedback) or decrease (negative feedback) production.","w":["Paracrine acts on neighbours.","Juxtacrine needs contact.","Not via blood.","Not."],"k":"Next lectures.","rn":[["","Autocrine binding that increases vs decreases molecule production?","Autocrine binding that increases vs decreases molecule production?","Increase: positive feedback; Decrease: negative feedback"]]},{"id":27100,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"Which of these best describes why gap junctions don't need receptors?","o":["They are physical pores","They bind hormones","They are GPCRs","They are nuclear","They are enzymes"],"e":"Physical pore – ions cross without ligand-gated or voltage-gated channels.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pores, not receptors.","rn":[["","What proteins form the gap junction pore?","What proteins form the gap junction pore?","Connexins"],["","Do GPCRs form an ion channel pore?","Do GPCRs form an ion channel pore?","No"],["","How wide is the gap junction pore?","How wide is the gap junction pore?","2-4 nm"]]},{"id":27101,"t":"Why Cells Communicate","s":"A tumour cell receives growth signals D and E inappropriately without need. What may result?","o":["Uncontrolled growth and division","Apoptosis of the tumour cell","Differentiation only","No change","Gap junction formation"],"e":"D + E signals → grow and divide; inappropriate signalling → pathology.","w":["Growth signals drive division.","F+G.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Growth signals → division.","rn":[["","What are the 7 reasons cells communicate?","What are the 7 reasons cells communicate?","Respond to immediate environment (incl. danger); Respond to central commands; Respond to local commands; Respond accordingly; Coordinate cellular responses; Integrate signals from multiple sources; Induce / decrease growth (division)"]]},{"id":27102,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about the term “ligand” definitions is correct?","o":["A molecule that binds a receptor","Only an agonist that activates the receptor","Only an antagonist","The receptor itself","A hormone only"],"e":"Different books vary; overall, a molecule that binds to a receptor.","w":["It covers any binding molecule.","Broader.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Ligand definition.","rn":[["","What is a ligand?","What is a ligand?","A molecule that binds to a receptor"]]},{"id":27103,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which receptor class is intracellular, used by steroid hormones such as cortisol?","e":"Nuclear receptors bind small hydrophobic ligands and act on genes over hours.","k":"Nuclear receptors.","a":["nuclear receptors","nuclear receptor","nuclear","intracellular receptor","cytoplasmic receptor"],"rn":[["","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Small molecules (e.g. nitric oxide); Hydrophobic molecules (e.g. steroid hormones, thyroxin)"],["","Cytoplasmatic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Cytoplasmatic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Signals that can cross the cell membrane"],["","What happens in the cell after a nuclear receptor is activated?","What happens in the cell after a nuclear receptor is activated?","Gene transcription; Protein synthesis"]]},{"id":27104,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which of these involves extracellular matrix interactions?","o":["Juxtacrine","Endocrine","Neuronal","Autocrine","Paracrine"],"e":"Juxtacrine: direct cell-to-cell or interactions with the ECM.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"ECM in juxtacrine.","rn":[["","How can a juxtacrine signal act through the extracellular matrix?","How can a juxtacrine signal act through the extracellular matrix?","Contact-dependent receptor-ligand binding with the ECM (extracellular matrix)"]]},{"id":27105,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about the role of physical junctions in communication is correct?","o":["Not the majority, but they help","They are the majority of signalling","They are absent in humans","They exist only in bacteria","They replace chemical signals"],"e":"Physical communication is not the majority but helps.","w":["Chemical is.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Physical: minority."},{"id":27106,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Platelet-derived growth factor is which type of paracrine signal?","o":["Protein","Gas","Amino acid derivative","Steroid","Sugar"],"e":"Proteins: cytokines, PDGF.","w":["NO.","Histamine.","Not.","Not."],"k":"PDGF protein.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of paracrine signals?","What are the 3 types of paracrine signals?","Proteins (e.g. cytokines, platelet-derived growth factor); Amino acid derivatives (e.g. histamine); Dissolved gas (e.g. nitric oxide)"]]},{"id":27107,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Why do cells need more signals to grow and divide than simply to survive?","o":["Division needs extra signals (D+E) on top of survival ones","Survival needs extra signals on top of division ones","One signal is enough for every outcome","Division happens without signals","Differentiation needs no signals"],"e":"Cells need multiple signals to survive (A+B+C), additional signals to grow and divide (D+E) or to differentiate (F+G). Deprived of survival signals, a cell undergoes apoptosis.","w":["Reverse.","Multiple signals.","It needs them.","F+G."],"k":"Rx-Bricks comments.","rn":[["","What happens to a cell deprived of survival signals?","What happens to a cell deprived of survival signals?","Apoptosis"],["","How do extracellular signals work together to control a cell?","How do extracellular signals work together to control a cell?","Cells often need several signals in combination; Some signals survive, others make it grow, divide or differentiate; Some signals inhibit behaviours or induce apoptosis"]]},{"id":27108,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which pairing of chemical signal and molecular class is correct list?","o":["Glucagon – peptide","Glucagon – steroid","Insulin – gas","Histamine – protein","Cortisol – peptide"],"e":"Messengers include proteins (insulin), peptides (glucagon, growth hormone), amino acid derivatives (histamine), lipid-derived hormones (steroids) and gases (NO, H₂S, CO).","w":["Peptide.","Protein.","Derivative.","Steroid."],"k":"Self-test slide questions.","rn":[["","What are 9 classes of signalling molecules?","What are 9 classes of signalling molecules?","Proteins (e.g. insulin); Peptides (e.g. glucagon, growth hormone); Sugars; Lipid-derived molecules; Hormones; Cytokines; Neurotransmitters; Pheromones; Gaseous molecules (NO, H2S)"]]},{"id":27109,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about apoptosis being normal is correct?","o":["Normal, when survival signals are removed","Always pathological, as cells should never die","It never occurs","It is caused only by drugs","It is caused by gap junctions"],"e":"Removing signalling so it's time for the cell to die – normal, non-pathological cell death.","w":["It is part of the normal life cycle.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Apoptosis normal.","rn":[["","What happens to a cell deprived of survival signals?","What happens to a cell deprived of survival signals?","Apoptosis"]]},{"id":27110,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about adrenaline receptors is correct?","o":["β1 (heart) and β2 (lungs) are both GPCRs","β1 (lungs) and β2 (heart) are both GPCRs","β receptors are ion channels","α receptors are nuclear","β1 is in the gut"],"e":"Both beta receptors are GPCRs; beta-1 heart, beta-2 lungs.","w":["Swapped.","GPCRs.","Not.","Not."],"k":"β1 heart, β2 lungs.","rn":[["","How does acetylcholine act on a heart pacemaker cell?","How does acetylcholine act on a heart pacemaker cell?","Binds M2 receptor (GPCR, a G protein-coupled receptor); Decreases rate of firing"]]},{"id":27111,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which statement about why chemical signals dominate and drugs are molecules is correct?","o":["We’re full of molecules; drugs mimic them","Drugs are mostly physical devices that act on junctions","Chemical signals are rare","Drugs use only gases","Drugs act via gap junctions only"],"e":"Chemical signals are the majority; drugs are molecules mimicking what we have.","w":["Drugs are mostly molecules.","Majority.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Drugs mimic molecules."},{"id":27112,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Antigen presentation requires direct contact via membrane-bound molecules on neighbouring cells. Name this signalling mode.","e":"Juxtacrine: contact between neighbours via membrane-bound molecules (and gap junctions, ECM interactions).","k":"Juxtacrine.","a":["juxtacrine","juxtacrine signalling","juxtacrine signaling","contact-dependent"],"rn":[["","What is juxtacrine signalling?","What is juxtacrine signalling?","Contact-dependent signalling; Immediate neighbours signal via membrane bound molecules"]]},{"id":27113,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which statement about neurotransmitter target cells is correct?","o":["Only the adjacent target cell","All cells in the body, via the blood after release","Via the blood","Only on themselves","Via gap junctions"],"e":"Neurotransmitters travel only across the synaptic gap to the adjacent target cell.","w":["They cross only the synaptic gap.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Neurotransmitter targets.","rn":[["","How far do neurotransmitters travel?","How far do neurotransmitters travel?","Only across the synaptic gap; To the adjacent target cell"]]},{"id":27114,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which links the hot plate example to the role of signalling?","o":["A signal to the brain makes you withdraw","Hot plates trigger hormones that move the hand","Gap junctions burn","Insulin is released","Cortisol causes withdrawal"],"e":"A signal sent to your brain (“this is hot… remove”).","w":["A nerve signal to the brain.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Hot plate → neural signal.","rn":[["","How do extracellular signals work together to control a cell?","How do extracellular signals work together to control a cell?","Cells often need several signals in combination; Some signals survive, others make it grow, divide or differentiate; Some signals inhibit behaviours or induce apoptosis"]]},{"id":27115,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"Which pairing of adrenaline receptor and vessel response is correct?","o":["Alpha – contraction of gut vessels","Alpha – relaxation of muscle vessels","Beta – contraction of gut vessels","Beta-1 – airway dilation","Beta-2 – increased heart rate"],"e":"Alpha → contraction (gut vessels); beta → relaxation (muscle vessels).","w":["Beta.","Alpha.","Beta-2.","Beta-1."],"k":"Alpha contraction.","rn":[["","Adrenaline: effect on smooth muscle of gut blood vessels?","Adrenaline: effect on smooth muscle of gut blood vessels?","Contraction (alpha receptors)"]]},{"id":27116,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Which of these hormones acts on a transmembrane receptor?","o":["Insulin","Cortisol","Oestrogen","Thyroxin","Progesterone"],"e":"Insulin acts on a transmembrane receptor; steroids/thyroxin on intracellular.","w":["Nuclear.","Nuclear.","Intracellular.","Nuclear."],"k":"Insulin transmembrane.","rn":[["","Insulin: where is it secreted from?","Insulin: where is it secreted from?","Pancreas"],["","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1: no insulin made; Type 2: absent or decreased response to insulin"],["","What are 2 examples of RTKs?","What are 2 examples of RTKs?","Insulin receptors; Growth factor receptors"]]},{"id":27117,"t":"G Protein-Coupled Receptors","s":"What does “metabotropic” mean for a receptor?","o":["GPCRs acting via second messengers","Ion channels (ionotropic)","Nuclear receptors","Gap junctions","Tight junctions"],"e":"GPCRs (metabotropic) – second messengers; ion channels (ionotropic).","w":["Ionotropic = ion channels.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Metabotropic = GPCR."},{"id":27118,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"What is the effect of activating kinase-linked receptors?","o":["Phosphorylation and transcription (hours)","Immediate ion flow within milliseconds of binding","Gap junction opening","Neurotransmitter release","Hormone secretion only"],"e":"Kinase-linked: protein phosphorylation, gene transcription, protein synthesis – hours.","w":["That is ion channels.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Kinase-linked effects.","rn":[["","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","What happens in the cell after a kinase-linked receptor is activated?","Protein phosphorylation; Gene transcription, then protein synthesis"]]},{"id":27121,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"Two patients have high blood glucose. Patient A, aged 12, has very low insulin levels in her blood because her pancreas no longer makes it. Patient B, aged 58, has normal or high insulin levels, but his muscle and fat cells take up little glucose in response.<br><br>Which signalling errors do these patients have, and which mode of signalling is affected?","o":["A: no signal made (type 1); B: decreased response to the signal (type 2); endocrine","A: decreased response (type 2); B: no signal made (type 1); paracrine","Both: no insulin made; autocrine","A: too many receptors; B: no receptors; juxtacrine","Both: blocked gap junctions between the pancreas and muscle cells, so insulin cannot pass between them"],"e":"Step 1: insulin is secreted from the pancreas into the bloodstream and acts on insulin receptors in distant cells to promote glucose absorption, which is endocrine (long-range) signalling. Step 2: in type 1 diabetes no insulin is made (no signal), while in type 2 diabetes there is an absent or decreased response to insulin by target cells.","w":["The two types are swapped, and insulin is endocrine.","Patient B has normal or high insulin.","The problems are lack of signal and lack of response.","Insulin travels in the blood, not through gap junctions."],"k":"Type 1: no insulin; type 2: reduced response; insulin = endocrine.","rn":[["","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1 vs type 2 diabetes: signalling error?","Type 1: no insulin made; Type 2: absent or decreased response to insulin"]]},{"id":27122,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A 30-year-old woman with asthma uses a salbutamol inhaler. Her airways open, but she also notices her heart racing. Her doctor explains that salbutamol binds some of the receptors that adrenaline uses, and these receptors differ between tissues.<br><br>Which receptors explain the wanted and unwanted effects?","o":["Wanted: beta-2 (airway dilation); unwanted: beta-1 (raised heart rate)","Wanted: alpha (airway dilation); unwanted: beta-2 (heart rate)","Wanted: beta-1 (airway dilation); unwanted: alpha (gut vessels)","Wanted: muscarinic; unwanted: nicotinic","Both from the same beta-2 receptors, as every cell in the body expresses the same set of receptors"],"e":"Step 1: different cells respond differently to the same signal depending on the set of receptors and the intracellular machinery they have, which explains why drugs sometimes have unwanted effects. Step 2: salbutamol helps dilate airways via beta-2 receptors, but can increase heart rate via beta-1 receptors, an unwanted effect.","w":["Alpha receptors contract gut vessels; beta-2 dilates airways.","Airway dilation is beta-2, not beta-1.","Salbutamol acts at adrenaline receptors, not acetylcholine receptors.","Different cells express different receptors."],"k":"Salbutamol: beta-2 airway dilation (wanted), beta-1 tachycardia (unwanted).","rn":[["","Salbutamol: which receptor causes increased heart rate?","Salbutamol: which receptor causes increased heart rate?","Beta-1"],["","Salbutamol: which receptor dilates airways?","Salbutamol: which receptor dilates airways?","Beta-2"]]},{"id":27123,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"A 65-year-old man gets chest pain on exertion from narrowed coronary arteries. He is given glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) spray, and his pain eases within minutes. His doctor explains that GTN works by supplying a gas that is normally made by nitric oxide synthase and diffuses into nearby smooth muscle cells.<br><br>Which mode of signalling does this gas normally use, and what is its target enzyme?","o":["Paracrine; guanylate cyclase in smooth muscle, causing vasorelaxation","Endocrine; insulin receptors in muscle","Juxtacrine; gap junctions in the heart","Autocrine; IL-1 receptors on monocytes","Paracrine; the glucocorticoid receptor in the nucleus, switching off genes for contraction"],"e":"Step 1: nitric oxide (NO) is produced by NOS and diffuses locally into nearby smooth muscle cells, which is paracrine (short-range) signalling; it has a short half-life. Step 2: NO activates its target enzyme, guanylate cyclase, causing vasorelaxation; medicinal nitrates such as GTN are used in angina to dilate coronary arteries.","w":["NO acts locally, not via the blood to distant cells.","NO does not need cell contact.","NO acts on neighbouring cells, not the cell that made it.","NO's target is guanylate cyclase, not GR."],"k":"NO: paracrine → guanylate cyclase → vasorelaxation; GTN for angina.","rn":[["","What is the target enzyme of NO in smooth muscle cells?","What is the target enzyme of NO in smooth muscle cells?","Guanylate cyclase"]]},{"id":27124,"t":"Types of Receptors and Ion Channels","s":"A pharmacologist measures how fast cells respond to four different signals. Signal 1 causes a response within milliseconds. Signal 2 acts within seconds. Signals 3 and 4 take hours; signal 3 binds a cell-surface cytokine receptor, while signal 4 is a steroid that enters the cell.<br><br>Which receptor types are these?","o":["1: ligand-gated ion channel; 2: GPCR; 3: kinase-linked; 4: nuclear","1: GPCR; 2: ion channel; 3: nuclear; 4: kinase-linked","1: nuclear; 2: kinase-linked; 3: GPCR; 4: ion channel","1: ion channel; 2: kinase-linked; 3: GPCR; 4: nuclear","All four are GPCRs, which act at every time scale from milliseconds to hours"],"e":"Step 1: ligand-gated ion channels (e.g. nicotinic ACh receptor) act in milliseconds and GPCRs (e.g. muscarinic ACh receptor) in seconds. Step 2: kinase-linked receptors (e.g. cytokine receptors) and nuclear receptors (e.g. oestrogen receptor, reached by hydrophobic steroids) act over hours.","w":["Ion channels are fastest (ms), GPCRs next (s).","Nuclear receptors are slow (hours).","GPCRs act in seconds, not hours.","There are four receptor types with different speeds."],"k":"Ion channel (ms) > GPCR (s) > kinase-linked ≈ nuclear (h).","rn":[["","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","What are the 4 types of receptors, quickest to slowest?","Ligand-gated ion channels; G protein-coupled receptors; Kinase-linked receptors; Nuclear receptors"]]},{"id":27125,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"A 40-year-old woman with a severe allergic reaction and a man with an autoimmune disease are both given a synthetic drug that crosses cell membranes and binds a receptor inside the cell. It switches some genes on and others off, producing anti-inflammatory and metabolic effects.<br><br>Which receptor does the drug act on, and which natural hormone does it mimic?","o":["The glucocorticoid receptor; cortisol","The insulin receptor; insulin","Beta-2 adrenoceptors; adrenaline","Guanylate cyclase; nitric oxide","A GPCR on the cell surface; glucagon, which is too large to cross the membrane"],"e":"Step 1: in the stress response, cortisol binds the glucocorticoid receptor (GR), which can both activate and suppress gene expression, with metabolic and anti-inflammatory effects; hydrophobic molecules like steroids cross the membrane to reach such receptors. Step 2: corticosteroids are synthetic compounds that activate GR and are used in autoimmunity, allergic reactions and asthma.","w":["Insulin acts on the insulin receptor to promote glucose uptake.","Adrenaline acts on adrenoceptors at the surface.","Guanylate cyclase is NO's target.","The drug binds a receptor inside the cell."],"k":"Corticosteroids activate GR (mimic cortisol): anti-inflammatory.","rn":[["","Which receptor does cortisol bind?","Which receptor does cortisol bind?","Glucocorticoid receptor (GR)"]]},{"id":27126,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"A woman is near the end of pregnancy. Studies of her uterine muscle show new channels between neighbouring muscle cells, formed by connexin proteins with 2–4 nm pores. These allow ions and small molecules to pass directly between cells. Similar channels are found between heart muscle cells.<br><br>What are these structures, and what is their role in each tissue?","o":["Gap junctions; coordinate uterine contractions and pass waves of excitation in the heart","Tight junctions; seal the uterus and heart","Desmosomes; link intermediate filaments for strength","Adherens junctions; link actin bundles only","Hemidesmosomes; link intermediate filaments to the basal lamina of the uterus and heart"],"e":"Step 1: gap junctions are channels between cells made by connexins with a narrow 2–4 nm pore, allowing diffusion of ions, nucleotides and sugars and providing rapid metabolic and electrical coupling. Step 2: they appear in the myometrium near the end of pregnancy to coordinate contractions during childbirth, and in the heart they let waves of electrical excitation pass quickly through the tissue.","w":["Tight junctions seal cells to prevent leakage.","Desmosomes link intermediate filaments but do not pass ions.","Adherens junctions link actin, not form pores.","Hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina."],"k":"Gap junctions (connexins, 2–4 nm): heart excitation, uterine coordination.","rn":[["","What do gap junctions do between heart muscle cells?","What do gap junctions do between heart muscle cells?","Let waves of electrical excitation pass quickly"],["","What do uterine gap junctions do during childbirth?","What do uterine gap junctions do during childbirth?","Coordinate uterine contractions"]]},{"id":27127,"t":"Modes of Intercellular Signalling","s":"A student classifies three signals. In signal 1, monocytes release interleukin-1 that binds receptors on the same monocytes. In signal 2, a sympathetic nerve releases noradrenaline that crosses only the synaptic gap to contract a blood vessel. In signal 3, an immune cell presents antigen to another by direct membrane contact, with no released molecule.<br><br>Which modes are these?","o":["1: autocrine; 2: neuronal; 3: juxtacrine","1: paracrine; 2: endocrine; 3: autocrine","1: juxtacrine; 2: paracrine; 3: endocrine","1: autocrine; 2: endocrine, because nerves can be very long; 3: juxtacrine","1: neuronal; 2: autocrine; 3: paracrine"],"e":"Step 1: autocrine signalling is when cells secrete molecules that bind their own receptors (IL-1 in monocytes), and neuronal signalling is when neurotransmitters cross only the synaptic gap to an adjacent cell (noradrenaline at adrenoceptors). Step 2: juxtacrine signalling is contact-dependent between immediate neighbours via membrane-bound molecules, with no released signal, as in antigen presentation.","w":["IL-1 binding its own cell is autocrine, and the nerve is neuronal.","Antigen presentation by contact is juxtacrine.","Neuronal signalling is short range, crossing only the synapse.","IL-1 on its own cell is autocrine."],"k":"Autocrine (own cell), neuronal (synaptic gap), juxtacrine (contact).","rn":[["","What are the 5 modes of intercellular signalling?","What are the 5 modes of intercellular signalling?","Endocrine; Paracrine; Neuronal; Autocrine; Juxtacrine; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":27128,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A pharmacology student compares two hormones. Hormone X is a large peptide that cannot cross the cell membrane, yet it still changes the activity inside its target cell. Hormone Y is a small hydrophobic steroid that acts by binding inside the cell.<br><br>Which statement best explains how each hormone acts?","o":["X binds a cell-surface receptor that signals inward; Y crosses the membrane to a cytoplasmic/nuclear receptor","X enters the cell by diffusion; Y stays outside","Both bind nuclear receptors after diffusing in","X acts only through gap junctions; Y acts only on GPCRs","X must be converted to a gas to cross the membrane; Y binds an ion channel on the cell surface"],"e":"Step 1: most signalling molecules are too large or hydrophilic to cross the membrane; they bind transmembrane receptors, and agonist binding changes the intracellular domains to start the response, so the molecule need not enter. Step 2: small and hydrophobic molecules (e.g. steroid hormones, thyroxin, NO) can cross the membrane and bind receptors in the cytosol or nucleus.","w":["Large peptides cannot diffuse across the membrane.","X cannot reach nuclear receptors.","X acts on surface receptors, and steroids act on internal receptors.","Peptides are not converted to gases."],"k":"Large/hydrophilic → surface receptors; small/hydrophobic → internal receptors.","rn":[["","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Which molecules can cross the membrane to reach cytoplasmic/nuclear receptors?","Small molecules (e.g. nitric oxide); Hydrophobic molecules (e.g. steroid hormones, thyroxin)"],["","Cytoplasmatic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Cytoplasmatic/nuclear receptors are for which signals?","Signals that can cross the cell membrane"],["","What are the 2 main locations of receptors?","What are the 2 main locations of receptors?","Transmembrane / cell surface; Cytoplasmic and nuclear"]]},{"id":27129,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A researcher adds drug P to cells and finds it binds a receptor and produces the same response as the natural signal. Drug Q binds the same receptor but produces no response, and when given first, it stops the natural signal from working.<br><br>Which terms describe drugs P and Q?","o":["P is an agonist; Q is an antagonist (blocker)","P is an antagonist; Q is an agonist","Both are agonists, as both are ligands","P is a hormone; Q is a cytokine","Both are antagonists, because any molecule that binds a receptor must inactivate it"],"e":"Step 1: a ligand is any molecule that binds a receptor, and an agonist binds and activates the receptor, as drug P does. Step 2: an antagonist (blocker) binds and inactivates the receptor, so drug Q blocks the response to the natural signal.","w":["The terms are swapped.","Both are ligands, but only P activates.","These terms describe signal types, not drug actions.","Agonists bind and activate receptors."],"k":"Agonist binds + activates; antagonist binds + inactivates.","rn":[["","What are 2 names for molecules that bind to and inactivate receptors?","What are 2 names for molecules that bind to and inactivate receptors?","Antagonists; Blockers"]]},{"id":27130,"t":"Intercellular Signals and Receptors","s":"A researcher studies a signalling pathway. A cell makes and releases a signal, which travels to a target cell and binds a specific receptor, changing the target cell's behaviour. The response continues for far too long when an enzyme that normally breaks down the signal is blocked.<br><br>Which step has been blocked, and why does the response continue?","o":["Removal of the signal, which normally terminates the response","Synthesis and release of the signal","Transport of the signal to the target","Detection by a specific receptor","The change in cellular behaviour, so the cell is unable to stop doing anything at all"],"e":"Step 1: chemical intercellular signalling usually involves synthesis and release, transport to the target, detection by a specific receptor, a change in cellular behaviour, and removal of the signal. Step 2: removal of the signal often terminates the cellular response, so blocking the breakdown enzyme means the response continues.","w":["The signal is made and released normally.","The signal reaches its target.","The receptor detects the signal.","The response itself occurs; the problem is it is not ended."],"k":"Signal removal terminates the response.","rn":[["","What does removal of the signal often do?","What does removal of the signal often do?","Terminates the cellular response"]]}]);
